# ============================================================
# PROJECT DEVELOPMENT RULES
# ============================================================
#
# This file is the PRIMARY SOURCE OF TRUTH for the project.
#
# Cline MUST read and follow this file before:
# - Creating any file
# - Modifying any file
# - Adding a component
# - Adding a page
# - Adding a feature
# - Changing UI
# - Changing project structure
# - Adding dependencies
# - Refactoring code
#
# Do not assume project conventions.
# Do not invent architecture.
# Do not introduce libraries or patterns without permission.
#
# ============================================================


# ============================================================
# 1. CORE DEVELOPMENT PHILOSOPHY
# ============================================================

The project must remain:

- Simple
- Readable
- Componentized
- Maintainable
- Beginner-friendly
- Easy to debug
- Easy to extend
- Consistent

Prefer simple code over clever code.

DO NOT write code merely because it is technically more advanced.

If two approaches solve the same problem:

    Choose the simpler approach.

The project is NOT intended to demonstrate advanced JavaScript,
advanced React patterns, complex abstractions, or unnecessary
architectural patterns.

The code should be understandable by a developer who knows:

- Basic JavaScript
- Basic React
- Basic HTML
- Basic CSS
- Basic API communication


# ============================================================
# 2. REQUIRED FIRST STEP FOR CLINE
# ============================================================

Before making ANY change, Cline MUST:

1. Read this `.clinerules` file.
2. Read `PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md`.
3. Inspect the existing project structure.
4. Identify existing components that can be reused.
5. Follow existing naming conventions.
6. Follow existing UI conventions.
7. Follow existing folder conventions.
8. Avoid creating duplicate functionality.

If the required information is not available:

DO NOT guess.

Instead:
- Inspect the project.
- Search existing files.
- Reuse existing patterns.
- Ask for clarification only if absolutely necessary.


# ============================================================
# 3. PROJECT TERMINOLOGY
# ============================================================

The file:

    PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md

contains:

- Naming conventions
- Domain terminology
- UI terminology
- Technical terminology
- Meaning of important project-specific words
- Component naming rules
- Variable naming rules
- API naming rules
- Database naming rules
- Words that MUST be used
- Words that MUST NOT be used

Cline MUST read this file before creating or naming:

- Components
- Pages
- Variables
- Functions
- API methods
- Routes
- Database entities
- UI sections
- Features


# ============================================================
# 4. CHANGE DOCUMENTATION — MANDATORY
# ============================================================

EVERY change made by Cline MUST be documented.

No change is considered complete until it is documented.

For every change, document:

1. What was changed
2. Why it was changed
3. Which files were created
4. Which files were modified
5. Which files were deleted
6. What functionality was added
7. What functionality was removed
8. Any API changes
9. Any database changes
10. Any UI changes
11. Any dependency changes
12. Any important implementation decision

Maintain:

    CHANGELOG.md

The changelog must contain a new entry for every meaningful change.

Use this structure:

    ## YYYY-MM-DD - Short Change Name

    ### Type
    Feature / Fix / Refactor / UI / Database / API / Other

    ### Summary
    Short explanation of the change.

    ### Files Created
    - path/to/file

    ### Files Modified
    - path/to/file

    ### Files Deleted
    - path/to/file

    ### Details
    - What changed
    - Why it changed
    - Important implementation details

    ### Testing
    - What was tested
    - Result

Keep changelog entries concise but complete.

DO NOT write vague entries such as:

    "Updated frontend."

Instead write:

    "Created reusable MonitorCard component and replaced duplicated
    monitor card markup on the dashboard."


# ============================================================
# 5. TAILWIND CSS — MANDATORY
# ============================================================

Tailwind CSS is the ONLY allowed styling system.

ALL UI styling MUST use Tailwind CSS utility classes.

Use:

    className="..."

Do NOT create custom CSS unless explicitly requested.

DO NOT create:

- New CSS files
- SCSS files
- LESS files
- Styled-components
- Emotion styles
- CSS-in-JS
- Inline style objects
- Another CSS framework

Do NOT introduce:

- Bootstrap
- Material UI
- Chakra UI
- Ant Design
- Bulma
- Semantic UI
- Any other UI framework

Tailwind must be the single styling approach.

Example:

GOOD:

    <div className="flex items-center gap-4 rounded-lg bg-white p-4 shadow">

BAD:

    <div className="monitor-card">

BAD:

    <div style={{ display: "flex", padding: "16px" }}>

BAD:

    import "./MonitorCard.css";


# ============================================================
# 6. TAILWIND STYLE RULES
# ============================================================

Use Tailwind classes directly in JSX.

Prefer readable className formatting.

Example:

    <div
        className="
            flex
            items-center
            justify-between
            rounded-lg
            border
            border-gray-200
            bg-white
            p-4
        "
    >

For short classes, a single line is acceptable.

Avoid extremely long unreadable className strings when splitting
them across lines improves readability.

Use existing project spacing, colors, borders, radius, shadows,
and typography patterns.

DO NOT randomly introduce new colors.

Before using a color:

1. Search the project.
2. Check existing Tailwind usage.
3. Reuse existing design patterns.

Consistency is more important than introducing new styling.


# ============================================================
# 7. NO ADVANCED JAVASCRIPT
# ============================================================

Use simple JavaScript.

The project must NOT use advanced JavaScript patterns unless
there is a clear requirement and the existing project already
uses them.

Prefer:

- const
- let
- functions
- simple arrow functions
- arrays
- objects
- map
- filter
- find
- basic conditionals
- basic loops
- async/await
- try/catch
- simple destructuring

Avoid unnecessary:

- Complex closures
- Generators
- Proxies
- Metaprogramming
- Advanced functional programming
- Complex currying
- Complex memoization
- Custom hooks for trivial logic
- Generic abstraction layers
- Complex state machines
- Unnecessary decorators
- Highly abstract utility systems
- Clever one-liners

If simple code works, use simple code.


# ============================================================
# 8. REACT RULES
# ============================================================

Use standard React patterns.

Prefer:

    Components
    Props
    useState
    useEffect
    useNavigate
    useParams

Use hooks only when necessary.

Do NOT create a custom hook for a small piece of logic that is
only used once.

Example:

GOOD:

    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

BAD:

    const useLoadingStateManager = (...) => {
        ...
    }

when the logic is only used in one component.

Keep React components understandable.


# ============================================================
# 9. COMPONENTIZATION
# ============================================================

The application MUST be componentized.

Do NOT create huge components.

A component should have one clear responsibility.

Example:

BAD:

    Dashboard.jsx

containing:

- Navbar
- Sidebar
- Monitor cards
- Charts
- Tables
- Modal
- API calls
- Form logic
- Validation
- Notification logic

Instead split it into logical components.

Example:

    Dashboard
        ├── DashboardHeader
        ├── MonitorSummary
        ├── MonitorCard
        ├── MonitorList
        └── RecentEvents


# ============================================================
# 10. COMPONENT CREATION RULE
# ============================================================

Create a separate file for a reusable component.

Example:

    components/
        MonitorCard.jsx
        StatusBadge.jsx
        Button.jsx
        Navbar.jsx

DO NOT put multiple large reusable components into one file.

Small helper components may remain together if there is a clear
reason.

When a component starts becoming difficult to understand,
consider splitting it.

Do not split components unnecessarily.


# ============================================================
# 11. COMPONENT RESPONSIBILITY
# ============================================================

Every component should have a clear responsibility.

For example:

    MonitorCard.jsx

should primarily handle:

- Displaying monitor information
- Displaying monitor status
- User actions related directly to that card

It should NOT also contain:

- Entire dashboard logic
- Authentication logic
- Large API systems
- Unrelated modal systems


# ============================================================
# 12. FILE NAMING
# ============================================================

Follow the naming rules in:

    PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md

General default:

React components:

    PascalCase

Examples:

    MonitorCard.jsx
    DashboardHeader.jsx
    StatusBadge.jsx

Pages:

    PascalCase

Examples:

    Dashboard.jsx
    Login.jsx
    Settings.jsx

Utility files:

    camelCase

Examples:

    formatDate.js
    apiClient.js

Do NOT randomly mix:

    monitor-card.jsx
    Monitorcard.jsx
    monitorCard.jsx

when the project convention requires:

    MonitorCard.jsx


# ============================================================
# 13. FOLDER STRUCTURE
# ============================================================

Follow the existing project structure.

Do NOT reorganize the entire project just because another
structure looks better.

If the project already has a structure, preserve it.

A recommended React structure is:

    src/
    ├── components/
    │   ├── common/
    │   ├── layout/
    │   └── monitor/
    │
    ├── pages/
    │
    ├── services/
    │
    ├── utils/
    │
    ├── hooks/
    │
    ├── assets/
    │
    ├── App.jsx
    └── main.jsx

However:

DO NOT force this structure if the existing project already
uses another sensible structure.

Consistency with the existing project takes priority.


# ============================================================
# 14. PAGE VS COMPONENT
# ============================================================

A PAGE represents a route/screen.

Examples:

    Login.jsx
    Dashboard.jsx
    Settings.jsx

A COMPONENT represents a reusable part of a page.

Examples:

    Navbar.jsx
    MonitorCard.jsx
    StatusBadge.jsx
    SearchBar.jsx

Do NOT create a page for something that should be a component.

Do NOT create a component for an entire route unless there is
a clear architectural reason.


# ============================================================
# 15. API / SERVICE CODE
# ============================================================

Keep API communication separate from UI components whenever
practical.

Do not fill React components with large API implementations.

Prefer:

    services/
        monitorService.js
        authService.js

Example:

    getMonitors()
    getMonitorById()
    createMonitor()
    updateMonitor()
    deleteMonitor()

Components should call service functions rather than containing
large repeated fetch implementations.

Keep API functions simple.


# ============================================================
# 16. FETCH / API RULES
# ============================================================

Use the project's existing API approach.

DO NOT introduce Axios if the project already uses fetch.

DO NOT introduce another HTTP library just because it is popular.

If fetch is already used:

    fetch()

should remain the default.

Handle:

- Loading state
- Success state
- Error state

Do not build an unnecessarily complex API abstraction.


# ============================================================
# 17. STATE MANAGEMENT
# ============================================================

Use local React state by default.

Prefer:

    useState()

Use context only when shared state genuinely requires it.

Do NOT introduce:

- Redux
- Zustand
- MobX
- Recoil
- Jotai

unless explicitly requested.

Do not introduce global state management for simple local state.


# ============================================================
# 18. FORMS
# ============================================================

Forms should remain simple.

Use:

- useState
- controlled inputs
- basic validation
- simple submit handlers

Do not introduce a form library unless explicitly requested.

Validation should be readable.

GOOD:

    if (!name.trim()) {
        setError("Name is required");
        return;
    }


# ============================================================
# 19. ERROR HANDLING
# ============================================================

Errors must be handled clearly.

Do not silently ignore errors.

Avoid:

    catch (error) {}

Prefer:

    catch (error) {
        console.error(error);
        setError("Something went wrong.");
    }

User-facing errors should be understandable.

Do not expose technical stack traces to users.


# ============================================================
# 20. LOADING STATES
# ============================================================

Async operations should have appropriate loading states.

Example:

    const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

Do not create complex loading-management systems.

Simple is preferred.


# ============================================================
# 21. REUSABILITY
# ============================================================

Reuse components when the same UI appears multiple times.

If the same markup appears in multiple locations:

1. Search for an existing component.
2. Reuse it if possible.
3. If no suitable component exists, create one.

DO NOT duplicate large blocks of JSX.


# ============================================================
# 22. DO NOT OVER-ENGINEER
# ============================================================

Before creating an abstraction, ask:

    "Does this actually solve a repeated problem?"

If NO:

    Keep the code simple.

Do not create:

    BaseComponent
    AbstractComponent
    GenericManager
    UniversalHandler
    ComponentFactory
    ServiceFactory

unless there is a real requirement.

Avoid architecture for architecture's sake.


# ============================================================
# 23. DEPENDENCIES
# ============================================================

Do NOT install a new npm package without checking whether the
existing project already provides the required functionality.

Before installing a package:

1. Check package.json.
2. Search existing source code.
3. Determine whether native JavaScript or existing libraries
   can solve the problem.
4. Only install if genuinely necessary.

Do not install packages for convenience alone.


# ============================================================
# 24. UI CONSISTENCY
# ============================================================

All UI must look like it belongs to the same application.

Reuse:

- Colors
- Typography
- Spacing
- Border radius
- Shadows
- Buttons
- Inputs
- Cards
- Status indicators

If an existing component already provides a style:

    REUSE IT.

Do not create a second visually similar component without reason.


# ============================================================
# 25. RESPONSIVENESS
# ============================================================

Use Tailwind responsive utilities.

Example:

    grid-cols-1
    md:grid-cols-2
    lg:grid-cols-3

Do not create custom media queries.

Use responsive Tailwind classes.

Do not optimize for every possible screen size.

Support reasonable:

- Mobile
- Tablet
- Desktop


# ============================================================
# 26. ACCESSIBILITY
# ============================================================

Use basic accessibility practices.

Buttons must be buttons.

Links must be links.

Inputs must have labels where appropriate.

Images should have meaningful alt text.

Interactive elements must be understandable.

Do not sacrifice accessibility for visual styling.


# ============================================================
# 27. COMMENTS
# ============================================================

Do not comment obvious code.

BAD:

    // Set loading to true
    setLoading(true);

Comments should explain WHY something exists when the reason
is not obvious.

GOOD:

    // Prevent duplicate requests while the monitor is being created.


# ============================================================
# 28. EXISTING CODE
# ============================================================

Before changing existing code:

1. Read the entire relevant file.
2. Understand its purpose.
3. Search where it is used.
4. Check whether other components depend on it.
5. Make the smallest safe change.

DO NOT rewrite an entire file when only a small change is required.


# ============================================================
# 29. DO NOT BREAK EXISTING FUNCTIONALITY
# ============================================================

Every change must preserve existing functionality unless the
user explicitly asks for that functionality to be changed.

Before modifying:

    Search references.

After modifying:

    Check affected components and routes.


# ============================================================
# 30. DUPLICATE CODE CHECK
# ============================================================

Before creating a new:

- Component
- Service
- Utility
- Hook
- Page

search the project first.

If something similar already exists:

    Reuse or extend it.

Do not create:

    MonitorCard.jsx
    ApiMonitorCard.jsx
    APIMonitorCard.jsx

when they represent the same concept.


# ============================================================
# 31. TERMINOLOGY RULE
# ============================================================

Use project terminology consistently.

For example, if the project defines:

    "Monitor"

as the term for a monitored API,

DO NOT randomly use:

    API
    Service
    Endpoint
    Check
    Job

as interchangeable terms.

If different terms have different meanings,
follow PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md exactly.


# ============================================================
# 32. "KIOSK" AND PROJECT-SPECIFIC WORDS
# ============================================================

Words such as:

    Kiosk
    Monitor
    Check
    Incident
    Workspace
    User
    Team
    Schedule

may have project-specific meanings.

Their meanings MUST be defined in:

    PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md

Cline must NOT assume the meaning of a project-specific term.

When adding a new domain term:

1. Add it to PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md.
2. Define its meaning.
3. Define where it should be used.
4. Define what terms it replaces or should not be confused with.
5. Then use the term in code.


# ============================================================
# 33. ROUTING
# ============================================================

Follow the project's existing routing system.

Do not introduce a second routing mechanism.

Routes should be simple and readable.

Example:

    /login
    /dashboard
    /monitors
    /settings

Do not create unnecessarily complicated nested routes.


# ============================================================
# 34. AUTHENTICATION
# ============================================================

Follow the existing authentication implementation.

Do NOT replace the authentication architecture unless explicitly
requested.

Do not duplicate authentication logic across components.

Authentication-related logic should remain centralized where
possible.


# ============================================================
# 35. SECURITY
# ============================================================

Never expose:

- Passwords
- Secret keys
- API secrets
- JWT secrets
- Database passwords
- Private credentials

Do not hardcode secrets.

Use the project's existing environment-variable approach.


# ============================================================
# 36. DATABASE / BACKEND CHANGES
# ============================================================

If the project contains backend or database code:

Before modifying it:

1. Inspect existing entities.
2. Inspect repositories.
3. Inspect services.
4. Inspect controllers.
5. Understand relationships.
6. Follow existing naming conventions.

Do not introduce a new architecture without necessity.

Keep backend code simple as well.


# ============================================================
# 37. DATABASE NAMING
# ============================================================

Follow existing database naming conventions.

Do not rename existing entities, fields, or tables unless required.

If a new database entity is needed:

1. Define its purpose.
2. Add terminology if required.
3. Follow existing entity naming.
4. Follow existing relationship patterns.
5. Document the change in CHANGELOG.md.


# ============================================================
# 38. TESTING
# ============================================================

After every meaningful change:

1. Check for syntax errors.
2. Check imports.
3. Check component references.
4. Check routes.
5. Check API calls.
6. Check obvious UI issues.

If tests exist:

    Run relevant tests.

Do not claim something was tested if it was not tested.


# ============================================================
# 39. BUILD / RUN CHECK
# ============================================================

Before declaring a feature complete:

Check that:

- The project builds.
- Imports resolve.
- Components render.
- Routes work.
- No obvious console errors exist.
- Existing functionality still works.

If something could not be tested, document it.


# ============================================================
# 40. FILE CREATION RULE
# ============================================================

Before creating a new file, ask:

    "Can the existing files handle this cleanly?"

If YES:

    Modify the existing file.

If NO:

    Create a new appropriately named file.

Every new file must have a clear responsibility.

Do not create files merely to reduce a few lines of code.


# ============================================================
# 41. DELETION RULE
# ============================================================

Never delete a file simply because it appears unused.

First:

1. Search the project for references.
2. Confirm it is genuinely unused.
3. Confirm no route or import depends on it.
4. Document the deletion.

Then delete it.


# ============================================================
# 42. REFACTORING RULE
# ============================================================

Do not perform large refactors while implementing a small feature.

Example:

If the user asks:

    "Add a delete button."

DO NOT:

- Rebuild the component architecture.
- Rename 30 files.
- Replace fetch with Axios.
- Introduce Redux.
- Replace Tailwind configuration.
- Rewrite routing.

Make the smallest change required.


# ============================================================
# 43. USER REQUEST PRIORITY
# ============================================================

Follow this priority:

1. Explicit user requirement
2. `.clinerules`
3. `PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md`
4. Existing project architecture
5. Existing code patterns
6. General best practices

If an explicit user request conflicts with these rules,
follow the explicit user request unless it creates a clear
technical or security problem.

Document significant deviations.


# ============================================================
# 44. BEFORE IMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST
# ============================================================

Before coding:

- Read `.clinerules`
- Read `PROJECT_TERMINOLOGY.md`
- Inspect project structure
- Search for existing implementation
- Identify reusable components
- Identify affected files
- Identify API changes
- Identify UI changes
- Identify terminology involved


# ============================================================
# 45. AFTER IMPLEMENTATION CHECKLIST
# ============================================================

After coding:

- Verify imports
- Verify component names
- Verify Tailwind classes
- Verify responsiveness
- Verify existing functionality
- Verify API calls
- Verify loading/error states
- Check for duplicate code
- Check for unnecessary dependencies
- Update CHANGELOG.md
- Confirm terminology remains consistent


# ============================================================
# 46. FINAL RULE
# ============================================================

KEEP IT SIMPLE.

The goal is NOT:

    "Most advanced implementation."

The goal is:

    "Clean, understandable, maintainable implementation."

When uncertain, prefer:

    Less code
    Fewer abstractions
    Fewer dependencies
    Smaller components
    Clear names
    Existing patterns
    Tailwind utilities
    Simple JavaScript
    Explicit logic


# ============================================================
# END OF .CLINERULES
# ============================================================